<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grigol Dadiani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1770">1770</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Grigol of Mingrelia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grigol Dadiani was Prince of Mingrelia in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, ruling with interruptions from 1788 to 1804. Contested by rival relatives and pressed by the King of Imereti, he placed his principality under the protection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in 1803. Mingrelia kept its own princes as Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> until its administration was taken over in 1857.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grigol was the eldest son of Katsia II Dadiani by his third wife, Anna Tsulukidze, and succeeded his father in 1788 at the age of 18. King David II of Imereti used the prince's youth to press his own claims and invaded Mingrelia, and Grigol withdrew to Lechkhumi, a highland district disputed between the two. King Heraclius II of Kartli-Kakheti intervened and helped defeat David at Matkhoji in July 1789, restoring Grigol and placing Solomon II on the throne of Imereti. Grigol then married Heraclius's granddaughter Princess Nino.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The quarrel over Lechkhumi soon returned. In 1791 Solomon II ousted Grigol in favour of his younger brother Manuchar, and Grigol was seized when he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> to negotiate. He escaped with the help of his uncle Giorgi Dadiani and Kaikhosro Gelovani, governor-general of Lechkhumi, held out in the fortress of Nogi and forced the king to recognise him again. He later had Gelovani, whose influence had grown, murdered in 1799. In 1802 Solomon defeated him at Salkhino and set up another brother, Tariel; Grigol regained power only by ceding the fort of Anaklia and sending his heir Levan as a hostage to Kelesh Ahmed-Bey Sharvashidze of Abkhazia.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed Kartli-Kakheti in 1801, Grigol approached the Russian commander Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-tsitsianov">Pavel Tsitsianov</a> in October 1802. The Treaty of Dadichala of 1 December 1803, ratified by Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> on 20 March 1804, recognised him as ruler of Odishi, Lechkhumi, Svaneti and Abkhazia under the Tsar's suzerainty, and he received the Order of Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>. He then asked the Tsar to depose Solomon II, a plan the Russian government at once disavowed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Grigol died unexpectedly at Muri in October 1804 and was buried at the Martvili Monastery. His widow Nino, who became regent for their son Levan V Dadiani, accused rival nobles of poisoning him, while her opponents spread rumours that she was behind it. Tsitsianov used the suspicion to remove the prince's doctor, the Italian Capuchin missionary Nicola di Rutigliano, whom he suspected of anti-Russian intrigues. Levan ruled Mingrelia until 1840.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grigol Dadiani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
